WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.690
This number of words you know
is sort of, uh, an indication of

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.070
your potential in the language.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.520
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here
today, and today, I'm going to talk

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.420
about something very important in
language learning, maybe in some

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.579
ways the most important thing: words.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.190
How Do we learn them?

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:18.870
Why are they important?

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:19.800
How do we use them?

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.820
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.290
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.980
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:29.930
I do appreciate it.

00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:36.614
So when I started with my son Mark into
LingQ and I was doing a lot of thinking

00:00:36.614 --> 00:00:41.655
about language learning, in fact, I did a
lot of research, uh, read books in English

00:00:41.655 --> 00:00:46.935
and Swedish and German in French on this
whole subject of language learning, I

00:00:46.935 --> 00:00:52.275
came to the conclusion that the single
most, I called it, the Gordian knot....

00:00:52.885 --> 00:00:58.125
The single simplest sort of
evaluation of where we are in a

00:00:58.125 --> 00:01:00.945
language is how many words we know.

00:01:01.305 --> 00:01:01.724
All right.

00:01:02.065 --> 00:01:07.605
Get a lot of criticism, people say, no,
you can be fluent with a few words, but

00:01:07.605 --> 00:01:09.225
in fact, you can't and I'll explain why.

00:01:09.884 --> 00:01:13.695
And so before I get going, though,
I want to show you that I don't just

00:01:13.695 --> 00:01:15.825
sort of rely on my own experience.

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:20.295
Although obviously, anybody talking
about say language learning is going

00:01:20.295 --> 00:01:21.705
to refer to their own experience.

00:01:21.994 --> 00:01:25.500
But I also did a lot of
research and I had a look...

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.810
you'll see behind me I
have all these books.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.649
I have CDs that I've listened to, but
I went and looked at my bookshelf here.

00:01:34.649 --> 00:01:40.080
So here's one of the books Learning
Vocabulary in Another Language ISP Nation.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:41.039
All right.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:43.910
I have lots of books on...

00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:45.810
second language acquisition.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.080
Rod Ellis, he does talk
about vocabulary acquisition.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:53.040
Second Language Vocabulary Acquisition.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.560
Norbert Schmitt, Vocabulary
in Language Teaching.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.470
And what have we got here?

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:02.700
Again Norbert Schmitt.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.880
Uh, this is from Cambridge
Description, Acquisition and Pedagogy.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.370
So it's not as if I haven't looked.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:14.460
I found that most of these books
were unnecessarily complicated.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.869
Again, I, I tend to look
for the Occam's raz...

00:02:21.329 --> 00:02:22.279
Occam's razor.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:26.970
In other words, the simplest explanation
of a phenomenon, the Gordian knot.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.715
And to me, words are the key sort
of measure of where we are in

00:02:32.715 --> 00:02:36.405
our language and how we acquire
them actually is quite simple.

00:02:37.035 --> 00:02:41.535
Um, there's a lot of talk about, you
know, you must read content that only

00:02:41.535 --> 00:02:47.235
has a small percentage of new words
in it, or that you have to meet a word

00:02:47.235 --> 00:02:49.245
so many times before you'll learn it.

00:02:49.515 --> 00:02:51.885
And all of that is almost irrelevant.

00:02:52.364 --> 00:02:57.270
To me learning words again, you'll not
be surprised to hear me say this is

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.610
all about acquiring them naturally.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.020
It's not because you say, I want
to learn 5,000 words that you're

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.340
going to learn 5,000 words.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.920
It's not because you deliberately
sit there with a stash of, of, uh,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.550
cards flashcards, or because you
read the dictionary that you're

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.080
going to acquire all those words.

00:03:17.174 --> 00:03:19.755
It may be something you enjoyed doing.

00:03:19.755 --> 00:03:24.494
It is a form of exposure to those
words, but ultimately it's going to

00:03:24.494 --> 00:03:28.815
be the amount that you are exposed
to the language in a meaningful

00:03:28.815 --> 00:03:31.484
environment that is pleasant to you.

00:03:31.575 --> 00:03:35.954
That is conducive to the brain
gradually acquiring these words.

00:03:35.954 --> 00:03:36.674
That's going to do it.

00:03:36.855 --> 00:03:40.785
It's a bit like if you go up to a girl
or if you're a girl, you go up to a guy

00:03:40.785 --> 00:03:43.515
or whatever you go and say I want to
be your girlfriend or your boyfriend.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:44.850
That's not going to do it.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.940
You actually have to, it's
more indirect than that.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.800
You can't just say, I want
to acquire all these words.

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:53.760
What happens is you're acquiring
them in the background.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:54.420
All right.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:59.490
And that's why when we created
LingQ, we wanted to have a measure

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.080
of the words you're learning.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.130
And I will show you a snapshot of all
the different languages that I have

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:08.820
learned on LingQ and how many words
I know in each of those languages.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.720
And that is a statistic
that's quite encouraging, but

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.760
it's not an absolute number.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:19.769
Um, I may know words in language, more
words in a certain language and in another

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:23.970
language, but because I'm working on, uh,
Persian right now, I can speak it better

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:29.775
than Czech erhaps, although I know far
more words in Czech than I do in Persian.

00:04:30.075 --> 00:04:33.494
Uh, if I were to go back to
Czech, I would quickly recover it.

00:04:33.645 --> 00:04:37.905
So that this number of words, you
know, is sort of, uh, an indication

00:04:37.905 --> 00:04:39.405
of your potential in the language.

00:04:39.585 --> 00:04:41.385
It's also relative.

00:04:41.565 --> 00:04:45.135
So in some languages like the
Slavic languages, there are many

00:04:45.135 --> 00:04:47.265
forms of essentially the same word.

00:04:48.044 --> 00:04:51.614
That means that the word count the way
we count them at LingQ where we count

00:04:51.674 --> 00:04:55.034
each form of the word as a different
word, that word count is going to

00:04:55.034 --> 00:04:59.895
be a bit bloated compared to other
languages where that's not the case.

00:05:00.315 --> 00:05:03.255
So it's not an absolute
number, but it's an indication.

00:05:03.284 --> 00:05:05.804
It's an indication that
you're making progress.

00:05:06.284 --> 00:05:09.135
You know, say six months
ago I knew 5,000 words.

00:05:09.224 --> 00:05:10.755
Now I know 20,000 words.

00:05:10.844 --> 00:05:15.534
So those moments when I feel I'm
not progressing in the language,

00:05:15.735 --> 00:05:17.455
the plateau, the doldrums.

00:05:17.695 --> 00:05:20.995
It reminds me that in fact, you
have acquired a lot of words.

00:05:21.205 --> 00:05:25.195
Now, some people say, what
does it mean to know a word?

00:05:25.255 --> 00:05:25.675
All right.

00:05:26.034 --> 00:05:30.505
Uh, you can't claim to know word
unless you can use it say some people.

00:05:30.625 --> 00:05:31.585
I don't agree with that.

00:05:32.094 --> 00:05:37.315
I am very much a proponent of
acquiring a large passive vocabulary.

00:05:37.795 --> 00:05:42.295
And if you understand a word in at
least one context, you know that

00:05:42.295 --> 00:05:43.815
word, at least for that context.

00:05:44.465 --> 00:05:48.450
If you come across it again, and it
doesn't make sense in that context

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.270
and you look it up again and you
find that there's another scope

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:53.430
of meaning for that word, fine.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:57.690
But passive vocabulary is what
it's all about because passive

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:03.630
vocabulary is the door to reading in
the language, understanding movies

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:07.030
in the language, having meaningful
conversations in the language.

00:06:07.485 --> 00:06:12.165
All of which opens you up to
meaningful, compelling input.

00:06:12.435 --> 00:06:15.675
Without the passive vocabulary,
you can't do those things.

00:06:15.825 --> 00:06:19.995
So I have always been against the
idea of trying to make sure that you

00:06:19.995 --> 00:06:25.425
can use every word you learn, go to
conversation class, try to use it.

00:06:26.715 --> 00:06:29.685
If I have an opportunity to use
whatever little I've learned

00:06:29.685 --> 00:06:31.035
in the language, I will do so.

00:06:31.515 --> 00:06:36.915
And typically, even at a relatively low
vocabulary level, I may start having

00:06:36.915 --> 00:06:41.565
sessions once or twice a week with a tutor
because it kind of helps me a little bit.

00:06:41.565 --> 00:06:48.285
It tends to make my input activities more
interesting for me, but fundamentally

00:06:48.285 --> 00:06:54.390
I rely on increasing my word count my
passive vocabulary because that's what's

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:56.160
going to enable me to learn the language.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:01.560
And if we acquire these words in what
I would call an honest way, which means

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:05.789
through massive listening and reading, we
are not only acquiring the words we are

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.040
acquiring familiarity with the language.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:12.600
Because again, it's a bit like the I've
often referred to sort of a dog races

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.590
where the dogs chase a mechanical rabbit.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.974
If we are trying to increase
our vocabulary, that drags

00:07:18.974 --> 00:07:20.234
us through all this content.

00:07:20.775 --> 00:07:24.044
And as we are listening and reading
our brain is becoming more and

00:07:24.044 --> 00:07:28.094
more familiar with the language,
increasing our comprehension level,

00:07:28.395 --> 00:07:32.715
increasing our passive vocabulary so
that we can then do more things in

00:07:32.715 --> 00:07:36.625
the language, including engage with
people in meaningful conversa...

00:07:36.645 --> 00:07:37.215
conversation.

00:07:37.424 --> 00:07:41.505
That's not to say that I
don't occasionally, you

00:07:41.505 --> 00:07:46.140
know, uh, do the flashcards.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:48.120
Predominantly phrases, by the way.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:53.160
I very much recommend doing flashcards
for phrases rather than individual words.

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:57.780
I will sometimes pick up interesting
dictionaries, for example, you

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:03.080
know, I like this, uh, you know...

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:09.150
so I can see German and Russian
side-by-side, it's just fun to do that.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:11.969
I don't know what it's doing
for me, but it's fun to do that.

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:12.849
Similarly.

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:14.219
I have one here for Portuguese.

00:08:15.179 --> 00:08:21.180
So I can see Portuguese and German
side by side, which is fun and

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:23.010
expands my sense of the language.

00:08:23.159 --> 00:08:25.105
Similar to here we have a...

00:08:25.105 --> 00:08:31.255
so I can see Russian
and Czech side by side.

00:08:31.255 --> 00:08:32.335
These are fun things to do.

00:08:32.365 --> 00:08:35.605
There's nothing wrong with doing
them, but fundamentally the

00:08:35.605 --> 00:08:38.335
words are acquired through input.

00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:44.190
And, uh, as we acquire these words,
we are acquiring the language.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:50.250
And so if your word count at LingQ
is now up to 10,000, 20,000, 30,000,

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.660
it's not necessarily something that
you can brag to other people about,

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:58.740
but it is an indication that you
are progressing in the language.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.480
Now I did make some notes here,
but I'm not going to worry about.

00:09:04.305 --> 00:09:06.645
Uh, I had a few other things that I
wanted to say, but I can't remember

00:09:06.645 --> 00:09:10.245
them, but I just want to leave you
with that thought that work on your

00:09:10.245 --> 00:09:13.214
vocabulary, work on words and phrases.

00:09:13.755 --> 00:09:17.805
Uh, if you have more opportunity to
speak and if you speak more, you'll

00:09:17.805 --> 00:09:22.155
speak better, more fluently, but
you have to have that basis in the

00:09:22.155 --> 00:09:27.074
language, which comes from having a
large passive vocabulary because the

00:09:27.074 --> 00:09:32.520
native speakers that you will encounter,
uh, either face-to-face individually

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:38.970
or with a group or in movies or books
or podcasts, they all have in almost

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:43.020
every case, a larger passive vocabulary
in your target language than you do.

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:46.000
And to understand them and
interact with them and, and

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:47.730
benefit from interacting with them.

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:49.890
You need that large passive vocabulary.

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:51.870
So there you have it, words.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:53.250
How we acquire words.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:57.579
It still comes back to
that basic massive input.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:09:58.910
Good luck.

00:09:58.935 --> 00:10:00.915
And I will leave you a couple
of videos that I have done

00:10:01.094 --> 00:10:02.834
previously on the same subject.

00:10:02.984 --> 00:10:04.905
Hopefully I don't contradict myself.

00:10:05.415 --> 00:10:05.745
Bye for now.

